Because antisymmetry is the mathematical formalization of orientation.

32:12.580 --> 32:16.560
It distinguishes between inside and outside, between clockwise and counterclockwise.

32:16.780 --> 32:17.760
Ah, I see.

32:17.840 --> 32:23.000
When you are measuring the flux of a field, say, energy radiating from a star, or the flow

32:23.000 --> 32:27.620
of the RSVP vector field across a boundary, you need to know if the slow is entering the

32:27.620 --> 32:28.620
region or exiting it.

32:28.720 --> 32:28.980
Right.

32:29.080 --> 32:31.320
The difference between flux in and flux out.

32:31.320 --> 32:36.940
The antisymmetry of the wedge product ensures that the orientation of the manifold is rigorously

32:36.940 --> 32:38.240
preserved in the integration.

32:38.240 --> 32:43.080
Okay, so we have built an incredibly robust cage of constraints here.

